 Data. Pf8 contains 33,325 samples and 12,493,205 variant sites. Ghana contributes 6,653 samples, of which 4,905 pass the release's own quality control. Isolates were collected between 2009 and 2021 and are geolocated only to administrative level 1, giving seven distinct coordinates across seven regions. A spatial join to a Ghana agro-ecological zone layer placed these 4,905 in four zones: Guinea savanna (n = 3,605), deciduous forest (n = 815), coastal savanna (n = 435) and transitional zone (n = 50); the smaller zone counts quoted under Methods are for the 1,900 isolates that survive this analysis's own sample QC. The Sudan savanna and the wet-evergreen and moist-evergreen forest zones are unrepresented. The zone layer itself has no citable published source: it is an unattributed re-host of an anonymous ArcGIS Online item, identified byte-for-byte by checksum, and is used here as accessed rather than as authoritative.
 Methods. Genotypes were obtained from the Pf8 Zarr store over HTTPS, restricted to Ghanaian samples, sites passing the release filter, biallelic single-nucleotide polymorphisms only (the ingest mask is FILTER_PASS & is_snp & numalt == 1), and the GT, AD, DP and GQ fields. Sample quality control applied a within-host fixation index (Fws) threshold of 0.95, a call-rate floor of 0.80, a heterozygosity outlier rule and clonal pruning; mixed calls were resolved to the majority allele by read depth and allele counts taken as one allele per isolate. Variant missingness is now recomputed after the sample cut rather than before it, which yields 3,916 SNPs across 1,900 isolates (mean missingness 2.95%): Guinea savanna 1,334, deciduous forest 337, coastal savanna 204, transitional zone 25. Analyses comprised three ordinations of the published Pf8 genotype-distance matrix (originally computed on the 4,905 release-QC-pass isolates and now re-run on the 1,900-isolate analysis set, by re-slicing the distance submatrix rather than reusing the cached full-set one), pairwise Hudson FST with a block jackknife, ADMIXTURE at K = 1-8 with cross-validation, Mantel tests of isolation by distance at zone and region level with a leave-one-population-out jackknife, TreeMix with 0-5 migration edges and jackknife edge standard errors, and a BayPass XtX scan calibrated against 100,000 pseudo-observed neutral SNPs with Benjamini-Hochberg q-values. Two further checks were added at final revision and both are reported below: a six-scenario threshold sensitivity analysis that rebuilds the analysis matrix under varied Fws and MAF cuts and recomputes pairwise FST and the region-level Mantel test in each, and a call-quality audit of the 19 XtX outliers against the other 3,897 scanned SNPs on the Pf8 annotation fields (MQ, VQSLOD, QD, FS, SOR, ReadPosRankSum, ExcessHet, InbreedingCoeff, culprit, RegionType). The resistance panel is separate and has been rebuilt from the codon up: 57 codons in nine genes were reconstructed from the CDS and the Pf3D7 reference genome - each codon mapped to its three genomic positions, every variant record at those positions collected regardless of numalt or is_snp, each isolate's alleles applied to the reference codon, and the codon translated - so that what is reported is the full residue spectrum including the reference residue, with Wilson confidence intervals and a four-zone chi-square test per residue. Mixed calls make a codon uncalled rather than being assigned a residue. Within-isolate haplotypes were counted on complete cases only; every call was compared one-for-one against MalariaGEN's published genotypes; and the pfk13 list was taken from the WHO compendium of validated and candidate markers, v1.0 (December 2025), rather than written by hand.
-Principal findings: population structure. Zone differentiation is very low, and after the sensitivity analysis this is the best-supported finding in the report. The largest pairwise FST is 0.00232 (SE 0.00053, 95% CI 0.00128-0.00336) between coastal and Guinea savanna, and one of six pairs - Guinea savanna versus the transitional zone (n = 25) - has a confidence interval spanning zero; the other five have strictly positive lower bounds. Varying the two thresholds that define the dataset leaves the maximum pairwise FST within 0.00204-0.00255 across all six scenarios, over sample sizes from 1,900 to 4,403 isolates and SNP counts from 3,275 to 10,876. Two directions within that range are worth recording: relaxing the Fws cut slightly raises FST (0.00232 to 0.00255 with no Fws filter at all), so polyclonal isolates were not masking differentiation, and tripling the SNP count at MAF >= 0.01 lowers it (0.00204), as expected if rarer variants are less differentiated between zones. Isolation by distance, by contrast, is not a robust result. At administrative-region level the test is significant at the published thresholds (Mantel r = 0.427, p = 0.025 with negative FST pairs floored to zero; r = 0.414, p = 0.041 without flooring the three negative pairs) and it is absent at zone level (r = 0.471, p = 0.122, significant in 0 of 4 leave-one-zone-out replicates, and with four populations admitting only 24 distinct label permutations). But across the six threshold scenarios the region-level test is significant in only two: the published thresholds and MAF >= 0.02 (r = 0.434, p = 0.047 censored, p = 0.050 uncensored, so that scenario is marginal in both directions). It fails at every relaxation of the Fws monoclonality cut (Fws >= 0.90, r = 0.318, p = 0.087; Fws >= 0.80, r = 0.256, p = 0.169; no Fws filter, r = 0.245, p = 0.179) and at MAF >= 0.01 (r = 0.432, p = 0.062). It is also significant in only four of seven leave-one-region-out replicates, collapsing when Upper East is dropped. The finding holds at the published thresholds and almost nowhere else, and is reported here as not robust - not as a supported result, and not as a weak but real distance effect. ADMIXTURE cross-validation error is minimised at K = 2 (0.83925) over K = 1 (0.84384), an improvement of 0.00459 on a near-flat curve. The K = 2 minor component is not uniform across zones (means 0.092 deciduous forest, 0.085 coastal savanna, 0.053 Guinea savanna, 0.042 transitional zone; Kruskal-Wallis H = 105.4, p = 1.1 x 10 to the -22). That is a real contrast - coastal and forest high, Guinea savanna and transitional low - concordant with the FST ranking but not a monotone latitudinal cline, and of negligible magnitude. TreeMix, now run with edge standard errors, supports exactly two migration edges (weights 0.152, p = 7.5 x 10 to the -4, and 0.805, p = 3.3 x 10 to the -16; variance explained 0.9969 at m = 2 against 0.8970 at m = 0); a third edge is indistinguishable from zero (p = 0.33).
