 6.9.12 Limitations of the panel
 [Observed] The zone contrasts in Table 6.8 inherit every design limitation of the FST analysis, including the one the original manuscript did not list. Zone is confounded with sequencing study. The confounding analysis is computed on the 4,905 QC-passing isolates, not on the 1,900-isolate analysis set, because study membership is a property of the full QC-passing cohort: those 4,905 come from 11 sequencing studies, of which Guinea savanna draws on six (n = 3,605), deciduous forest on five (n = 815, of which four, n = 811, are large enough to enter the within-zone test — the fifth is a 4-isolate study), coastal savanna on two (n = 435) and the Transitional zone on a single study (1083-PF-GH-CONWAY, n = 50); 435 + 815 + 3,605 + 50 = 4,905. Four of the six zone pairs share no partner study at all, among them coastal savanna against Guinea savanna, which is the pair carrying both the maximum FST and every marker contrast reported above. Batch effects within these data are demonstrable rather than hypothetical — holding zone constant, call rate differs between studies within Guinea savanna at p = 2.8e-197 (Kruskal–Wallis, 6 studies, n = 3,605) and within deciduous forest at p = 7.7e-88 (4 studies, n = 811). A zone difference in marker frequency and a study difference in marker frequency are not separable here for the pairs that carry the largest effects.
