 9.2 On the design limitations specifically
 [Interpreted] Limitations 1, 2 and 3 are not analytical shortcomings that better methods would overcome. They are properties of the Pf8 metadata and of how Pf8 was assembled. No reanalysis of these data can produce a zone effect separable from a region effect, because for three of the four zones there is only one region in each; and no reanalysis can separate the Coastal–Guinea savanna contrast from a sequencing-study contrast, because no study contributed isolates to both. This should be stated plainly in any write-up rather than managed with hedging language, and it bears directly on how the thesis's objectives can be framed. Limitation 3 has now produced a concrete casualty rather than a theoretical one: the isolation-by-distance result it constrains did not survive threshold variation (§9.1, Table 8.3).
 [Interpreted] Limitation 8 is of a different kind and deserves separating from the rest. It was not a property of the data but of the code, it was invisible to every check that looked only at this project's own outputs, and it was corrected only because an external reader asked whether the marker list was the right list. Unlike limitations 1 to 3 it is fixable, and it has been fixed; unlike them it was also, while it lasted, producing confidently stated numbers that were wrong. The appropriate response in a write-up is not a hedge but a statement of what the panel now does and how it was checked against something outside the pipeline (§8.1, rows 14 to 18).
