 Two incidents during this revision's runs, recorded because both bear on data integrity. (i) A results file was destroyed and restored. results/baypass/xtx_all_snps.csv was found at 0 bytes, truncated at 08:29:53 — hours after the 01:42 run that produced it, so almost certainly a stray shell redirect where a read was intended during a verification pass. It was the only zero-byte file under results/ or interim/. It was regenerated from the intact BayPass outputs, and the regeneration is provably faithful: outlier_snps.bed, xtx_calibration.json and xtx_threshold.txt are byte-identical by md5 before and after, and the restored file carries the same 3,916 rows and the same 19 outliers. The consequence is in fact positive — the per-SNP q-values are on disk again, so the pfaat1 q-value of 0.0049 and the tie between the two AAT1 SNPs are now reproducible from a primary output rather than from a narrative (§7.5 note). But the episode is also the clearest argument this project has produced for version control, which is gap (ii) in the paragraph below: nothing in the setup would have detected the truncation except someone opening the file. (ii) A log misreported its own output path. The --suffix patch to step03_umap.py suffixed the file writes but not the print statements, so the stage 3 re-run reported writing to the published dimred_comparison.csv when it had correctly written dimred_comparison_qcset.csv. No data were at risk — verified from the published file's mtime and contents — but a log that misstates where it wrote is exactly what causes a later misattribution, and this is one more instance of the pattern in §8.1: the defect was inside a correction, not inside the original code. Fixed.
